---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import HtmlEntityEncoder from '@/components/tools/dev/HtmlEntityEncoder.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="html-entity-encoder">
  <HtmlEntityEncoder client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to encode and decode entities</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>Encode</strong> to turn characters into entities, or <strong>Decode</strong> to turn entities back into characters.</li>
      <li>Pick the entity style: named (<code>&amp;mdash;</code>), decimal (<code>&amp;#8212;</code>) or hexadecimal (<code>&amp;#x2014;</code>).</li>
      <li>Choose how much to escape. <strong>Only special characters</strong> is correct for almost all modern pages.</li>
      <li>Copy the result, or use the reference table to look up a single character.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Which characters actually need escaping</h2>
    <p>
      HTML needs escaping only where a character would otherwise be read as markup, and that depends on
      where the text is.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Context</th><th>Must escape</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Element text</td><td><code>&amp;</code> and <code>&lt;</code></td><td><code>&lt;</code> opens a tag; <code>&amp;</code> starts an entity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Double-quoted attribute</td><td><code>&amp;</code> and <code>"</code></td><td>The quote would close the attribute early</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Single-quoted attribute</td><td><code>&amp;</code> and <code>'</code></td><td>Same reason</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unquoted attribute</td><td>Almost everything</td><td>Do not use unquoted attributes with dynamic values</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Most libraries escape all five of <code>&amp; &lt; &gt; " '</code> in all contexts, because it is cheap
      and keeps a value safe if it moves to another context later. Minimal mode here does the same.
    </p>

    <h2>Named, decimal or hexadecimal?</h2>
    <p>
      All three give the same character. Named references are readable: <code>&amp;nbsp;</code> tells you
      more than <code>&amp;#160;</code>. But there are over 2000 of them, only HTML defines them (XML has
      five), and older parsers disagree on edge cases. Numeric references work everywhere and need no lookup
      table. Use hexadecimal when you copy a code point from the Unicode charts, where it is written as
      <code>U+2014</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your page is served as UTF-8, as it should be, write most characters directly. <code>&mdash;</code>
      is 3 bytes in UTF-8 and <code>&amp;mdash;</code> is 7. The character is also easier to read in source
      and you can search for it in your editor.
    </p>

    <h2>Entities you will actually meet</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Character</th><th>Named</th><th>Numeric</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>&amp;</td><td><code>&amp;amp;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#38;</code></td><td>Escape it first, or you will double-encode everything else</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&lt;</td><td><code>&amp;lt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#60;</code></td><td>The one that actually opens a tag</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&gt;</td><td><code>&amp;gt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#62;</code></td><td>Not strictly required in text, escaped by convention</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"</td><td><code>&amp;quot;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#34;</code></td><td>Required inside double-quoted attributes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>'</td><td><code>&amp;apos;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#39;</code></td><td><code>&amp;apos;</code> is HTML5 and XML; older HTML only had the numeric form</td></tr>
        <tr><td>non-breaking space</td><td><code>&amp;nbsp;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#160;</code></td><td>Prevents a line break; not the same character as a normal space</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&mdash;</td><td><code>&amp;mdash;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#8212;</code></td><td>Em dash</td></tr>
        <tr><td>–</td><td><code>&amp;ndash;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#8211;</code></td><td>En dash, for ranges</td></tr>
        <tr><td>…</td><td><code>&amp;hellip;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#8230;</code></td><td>A single ellipsis character, not three dots</td></tr>
        <tr><td>©</td><td><code>&amp;copy;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#169;</code></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>€</td><td><code>&amp;euro;</code></td><td><code>&amp;#8364;</code></td><td></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Escaping is not the whole of XSS defence</h2>
    <p>
      HTML escaping stops text from being read as markup, which blocks the classic
      <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> injection into element text. It does not protect other contexts. A value
      inside a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block needs JavaScript string escaping. A value used as an
      <code>href</code> needs a scheme allowlist, because <code>javascript:</code> URLs are not markup and
      HTML escaping does not change them. A value inside a <code>style</code> attribute needs CSS escaping.
      Use a templating engine that knows the context, and use this tool to debug, not as a security control.
    </p>

    <h2>Astral characters and emoji</h2>
    <p>
      JavaScript stores characters above U+FFFF as two UTF-16 code units. Encoders that iterate over code
      units split them and output two meaningless references. This tool iterates by code point, so 😀
      becomes the single reference <code>&amp;#128512;</code> and decodes back to the same emoji.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
